;  382
;  383    ! EXACT_TEXT is X in the fewest significant digits that read back as X
;  384    ! itself: 2.5, 0.1, 0.3333333333333333, 1e-20, 6.02214076e+23, the
;  385    ! exponent written as fcalc shows one.
;  386    !
;  387    ! A REAL(RK) has 53 bits, about 15.95 decimal digits: 15 digits don't
;  388    ! always tell two neighboring values apart, and 17 always do. So X is
;  389    ! written in scientific notation with 15 digits, then 16, then 17,
;  390    ! until reading the digits back (by the same list-directed READ that
;  391    ! FCLEXER uses for numbers typed) gives X again. Both machines convert
;  392    ! decimal to binary and back with correct rounding, so both write the
;  393    ! same digits for the same value.
;  394    !
;  395    ! ESw.dE3 writes a number with one digit before the point, d after it, and
;  396    ! an exponent of three digits: 3.333E-001. The format itself is made at
;  397    ! run time by an internal WRITE, since d changes: a format may be any
;  398    ! character expression, not only a constant.
;  399    function exact_text(x) result(text)
	.ENTRY	FCSTATE.EXACT_TEXT,^M<R2,R3,R4,R5,R6,R7,R8>
